{0 Reference Verification Architecture}

This page records the intended separation between the semantic verification
kernel and implementation choices such as sharing, slicing, batching, dumps,
Why3 emission, SMT calls, and profiling.

The dependency-layer check described in [docs/architecture_layer_rules.md] is
necessary but not sufficient. It verifies that libraries point in the right
direction. It does not say which transformations are part of the correctness
argument and which transformations are only representation or backend
optimizations.

{1 Architecture Type}

Kairos uses a ports-and-adapters architecture with a layered domain:

- incoming adapters parse and elaborate the source language;
- the domain core contains the elaborated language and shared IR datatypes;
- the verification domain consumes supplied automata to construct product proof
  data;
- the proof-export domain builds the exchange view used by diagnostics and
  future Rocq synchronization;
- outgoing adapters render artifacts or call external tools;
- the composition layer wires concrete adapters to application use-cases.

This structure should be preserved. The cleanup target is not to replace it,
but to make the semantic boundary explicit inside it.

{1 Reference Boundary}

The reference verification kernel starts after frontend elaboration and after
the property automata have been supplied:

{[
Verification_model.program_model
  + supplied assumption and guarantee automata
  -> product states
  -> product steps
  -> generated proof-kernel clauses
  -> relational obligations
]}

This path must be deterministic and independent from:

- Spot invocation;
- Why3 or Z3;
- backend grouping thresholds;
- textual WhyML size optimizations;
- worker scheduling;
- dump generation;
- profiling counters.

Spot may be used to produce automata, but the reference kernel consumes
automata as parameters. Rocq should therefore synchronize with the supplied
automata and the generated kernel obligations, not with Spot itself or with a
formalization of the LTL-to-automata translation.

{1 Current Pipeline Map}

The current implementation roughly has the following stages:

- surface parsing and elaboration:
  [Kx_surface_syntax], [Kx_elaborate], [Kx_ast], [Kairos_to_model];
- core semantic model:
  [Verification_model], [Core_syntax];
- automata construction:
  [Automata_generation] in [kairos_runtime_automata], wired to Spot from
  runtime orchestration;
- reference product construction:
  [Product_build], [Temporal_automata], [From_model],
  [Orchestration.build_reference_product];
- proof-kernel export:
  [Proof_kernel_types], [Proof_kernel_product],
  [Proof_kernel_generated_clauses], [Proof_kernel_clause_lowering],
  [Proof_kernel_step_summaries], [Proof_kernel_pass];
- proof/IR shaping passes:
  [Pre], [Product_reachability], [Post], [Temporal_lower],
  [Formula_sharing];
- runtime/backend orchestration:
  [Pipeline_build] and [Runtime_snapshot] in [kairos_runtime_core],
  external automata production in [kairos_runtime_automata], [Proof_runner]
  in [kairos_runtime_proof], diagnostics in [kairos_runtime_diagnostics],
  and the public facade in [kairos_verification_runtime];
- Why3-specific representation and emission:
  [Why_runtime_view], [Why_compile], [Why_compile_step],
  [Why_text_render], [Why_pipeline].

The current technical debt is that some stages named as domain passes still
contain representation or cost decisions, and some backend views perform
simplifications rather than only projecting data to Why3.

{1 Pass Classification}

Every pass should be classified before it is moved or optimized:

- {b reference}: part of the semantic construction mirrored by Rocq;
- {b reference_normalization}: deterministic semantic normalization required
  by the reference view;
- {b obligation-preserving optimization}: grouping, sharing, slicing, or cuts
  that must preserve generated obligations;
- {b backend}: target-specific representation, command invocation, proof
  result collection, and reporting;
- {b external}: Spot, Why3, Z3, Graphviz, timing services, and filesystem
  artifact generation.

The rule is:

{[
reference output is stable under backend options
]}

Changing a sharing threshold, worker count, dump option, or Why3 grouping mode
must not change the canonical product steps or canonical proof-kernel clauses.

{2 Current Classification}

The current pass classification is:

- [Pre] is {b reference}. It adds [propagation_requires], local [requires],
  assumption/program guard requirements, stability requirements, and initial
  invariant goals. These facts shape the proof obligations for a product step.
- [Product_reachability] is {b reference}. Despite its name, it must not be
  treated as pruning. The current pass adds obligations such as
  [guard => false] for destinations classified as unreachable by the finite
  analysis. Until this is replaced by a more general certified reachability
  invariant, it belongs to the correction story.
- [Post] is {b reference}. It adds the safe-successor disjunction, destination
  invariant obligations, guarded destination invariant obligations, and
  product-characteristic preservation facts.
- [Temporal_lower] is {b reference_normalization}. It computes the
  [temporal_layout] and rewrites [pre]/[pre_k] occurrences through explicit
  temporal bindings. This is a semantic normalization that Rocq must see.
- [Formula_sharing] is {b obligation-preserving optimization}. It only replaces
  structurally equal top-level formulas by shared physical representatives.
  It must not simplify, add, or remove obligations.

The machine-readable version of this classification is
[docs/reference_pipeline_boundaries.json]. The check
[scripts/check_reference_pipeline_boundaries.py] rejects missing stages,
unknown classification names, and stages left as [to_classify].

{1 Rigorous Architecture Modeling}

Architecture should be represented at three complementary levels.

First, use diagrams for orientation, not as the source of truth:

- a C4-style context diagram for users, Kairos, Rocq, Spot, Why3, and Z3;
- a container/component diagram for frontend, domain core, verification
  kernel, proof export, runtime orchestration, and backends;
- a pipeline diagram showing data artifacts and pass boundaries.

Second, keep a versioned manifest as the source of truth for architecture
classification. The manifest should record:

- stage name;
- classification vocabulary;
- owned paths;
- inputs and outputs;
- allowed effects;
- forbidden effects;
- Rocq synchronization role.

Third, enforce the manifest with scripts. Checks should fail when:

- a reference stage imports or mentions external-tool adapters;
- a pass remains unclassified;
- a path in the manifest disappears;
- backend options affect canonical kernel output;
- a reference pass starts deleting product cases without an explicit certified
  reachability argument.

The current regression for the fourth point is
[tests/check_reference_stability.sh]. It compares the normalized program and
proof IR dumps produced for the same source program under different
backend-only options. This test deliberately does not vary source-level or
proof-shaping options such as public guarantee grouping.

For design history, use short Architecture Decision Records. An ADR should
record one decision, its alternatives, and the invariant that must survive
future refactors. The manifest says what the architecture is; ADRs say why it
became that way.

{1 Rocq Synchronization Contract}

Rocq should track a versioned kernel exchange format. The best current
candidate is [Proof_kernel_types.node_ir] plus the exported summary payload.

The exchange format should become the contract:

{[
kernel schema version
  + normalized reactive program
  + assumption automaton
  + guarantee automaton
  + explicit product states
  + explicit product steps
  + generated clauses
  + relational clauses
  + temporal layout
]}

When Rocq evolves, the boundary adapter should evolve with it. Why3 backend
thresholds and external tool orchestration should not need to be reflected in
the Rocq development.

{1 Optimization Discipline}

Optimizations should live after the reference kernel, or be marked explicitly
as normalization with a simple proof obligation.

Acceptable optimization shapes include:

- replacing repeated formulas by definitions;
- grouping conclusions that already share the same source precondition;
- introducing explicit local cuts and their proof obligations;
- choosing a backend-specific representation for already generated facts;
- scheduling independent obligations in parallel.

The following are not optimizations unless justified by a separate certified
argument:

- deleting product edges;
- filtering mixed-phase guards by syntactic contradiction;
- shifting program guards or assumption guards into later ticks;
- hiding reachability assumptions in the backend;
- changing generated product obligations because a backend cannot prove them.

The phase discipline behind these rules is described in
{!page-temporal_phase_semantics}. The current product-proof status is recorded
in {!page-product_proof_pipeline_audit}. The performance motivation for
sharing and grouping is recorded in {!page-pipeline_cost_model}.

{1 Cleanup Plan}

The cleanup should be done in this order:

1. keep the existing layer rules, but add a reference-boundary manifest;
2. expose a named reference-product entry point in the domain orchestration;
3. classify every pipeline pass in the manifest before moving code;
4. make the proof-kernel export the versioned Rocq synchronization point;
5. move Why3-only simplification/slicing behind explicit backend pass names;
6. keep profiling and dump decisions out of the reference data path;
7. add regression checks that canonical kernel output is unchanged by backend
   options;
8. only then resume performance work on grouped or shared obligations.

This ordering avoids a large mechanical rewrite while still making the
correction-critical path visible.
